README-GREP -- six ways to search a file, and which to takeShort answer: take `grep'.  It is the only one of the six that needs noMicroware module, it has six files of documentation on this disk, and it isthe one whose regular expressions everything else you know will match.Take `bm' as well if you search big files often: it is 6,834 bytes againstgrep's 72,364 and uses Boyer-Moore, which skips rather than scanning.THE SIX-------  file      bytes  cio  module   docs      what it is  ---------------------------------------------------------------------  grep      72364  no   grep     6 files   GNU grep -- regular expressions  ggrep     53124  yes  ggrep    --        an earlier GNU build  fgrep     19960  yes  fgrep    3 files   FIXED strings, no expressions  bm         6834  yes  bm       1         Boyer-Moore, fast, fixed string  lgrep      2446  yes  lgrep    1         the smallest thing that searches  gep        3654  yes  gep      --        expressions from a FILEEvery one of these except `grep' needs `cio'.  It ships here, so on thisdisk that costs nothing; it is one more file to carry if you take one away.DOC/README-CIO.TAKE THIS IF------------  grep    you want one and no decisions.  Regular expressions, `-c' to          count, `-v' to invert, and the documentation is here.  bm      you are searching something large for a FIXED string.  It is a          tenth of grep's size and it answers in its exit status when there          is no match, which is what a script wants.  Measured 2026-09-01:          `bm apple file' prints every matching line and exits 0; a pattern          that is not there prints nothing and exits 1.  fgrep   the same job as bm, with three files of documentation and three          times the size.  lgrep   you are desperately short of room.  2,446 bytes.  ggrep   only if `grep' will not run for you -- it is the older build of          the same program and needs cio where grep does not.  gep     you have MANY patterns.  It is the odd one of the six: the          expressions come from a FILE named with `-e', not from the          command line, and its own option list says `(required)'.          `gep -e=<patternfile> <sourcefile>'.  Handing it a pattern and a          file the way you would grep earns `gep: more than one path          specified' -- measured 2026-09-01 and asserted in          `tools/datatests/text3.cases'.IF YOU CAN TAKE ONLY ONE FILE`grep', 72,364 bytes, and it is the only one that needs nothing else.If 72K is too much: `bm', 6,834 bytes -- but it takes a fixed string, not anexpression, and it needs `cio'.NO MODULE NAME COLLIDESAll six report their own filename, so you may load as many as you liketogether.  That is not true of every family here; DOC/README-KERMIT has onethat does collide.